    "done_when": "The pending-plan limit becomes a warning, per the relocked decision wip-limit-mode = warn.\n\nreckon/mcp.py:_edit_plan with create=True always creates the plan. When the project declares a limit and its pending count is at or above it, the success response carries a warning field. The field names the limit, the pending count and the three pending plans nearest to closing, ranked by impl, highest first, with the same wording the refusal used. Under the limit, or in a project declaring no limit, the response carries no warning field.\n\nThe override_wip_limit argument, the refusal branch and the override comment are removed. The tool docstring and the MCP op vocabulary describe the warning, not a refusal, and no dead parameter is left behind.\n\ntests/test_pending_plan_limit.py is rewritten to the new rule, keeping the synthesised project under tmp_path. It asserts:\n- a create at the limit succeeds and carries the warning naming the three nearest plans;\n- a create under the limit carries no warning;\n- a project declaring no limit carries no warning;\n- no plan comment is written in any case.\n\nThat file and tests/test_edit_plan.py, tests/test_edit_plan_append_evidence.py and tests/test_edit_plan_section_writes.py pass at exit 0. The manifest writes baseline_suite and after_suite each as one line of armed JSON, and every gate log opens with one line naming its revision, tree and command and ends with EXIT=. The declared negative control is applied and observed to fail, and its log's absolute path is in the manifest with an EXIT= line and the FAILED id. This node is small: one landing: line and no evidence prose.",
    "estimated_hours": null,
    "goal": "Turn the pending-plan limit from a refusal into a warning on the created plan's response, per the lead's relocked decision",
